{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he asked, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>who were with him in custody</b>, and also why it was necessary to say <b>in his master's house</b>?" "<b>And it will appear</b> that Scripture gives a reason for Joseph's asking the two officers ״מַדּוּעַ פְּנֵיכֶם״, for this is not among the laws of proper conduct — to seek to know the thoughts of great men. And this is its saying that <b>Joseph asked</b> <b>Pharaoh's officers</b> — meaning, even though he, Joseph, was as known a servant, and they were the king's officers, nevertheless he asked them. And the reason is ״who were with him in custody״ — there are two reasons in this: the first, that because they were with him in prison, he was concerned that their anger was directed at him; and the second, that since they were with him in prison, in every respect they were equal and all were considered prisoners, and for this reason he paid no mind to their greatness. And further, that they were in his master's house, and it was possible that this anger affected his master, so he inquired — the duty of a faithful servant toward his master to be vigilant in his affairs — and therefore he asked them, saying to them ״מַדּוּעַ״ etc." "He was precise in saying <b>today</b>, for one cannot say that their faces were downcast because of their imprisonment in the house of custody, since this matter is not new, and they would have had to be so both yesterday and the day before."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:16.186411640Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}